# Power BI Penetration Test Methodology

> **Version**: 0.1 (research draft — iterate after each engagement)
> **Constraints**: Target is inside a private network. All testing performed from an authorized corporate workstation using **only**: web browser (Edge/Chrome + DevTools), PowerShell 5.1+ built-ins (`Invoke-RestMethod`, `Get-Service`, `Get-CimInstance`, `Expand-Archive`, etc.), and CMD built-ins. **No tool downloads.**
> **Role split**: The operator (you) executes on the private network. The assistant (me) designs tests, interprets output, and adjusts the plan.

---

## 1. Power BI Attack Surface Model

A Power BI estate exposes six distinct attack surfaces. Every engagement should map which of these exist before deep testing:

| # | Surface | What it is | Typical findings |
|---|---------|------------|------------------|
| S1 | **Power BI Service (cloud)** | app.powerbi.com — workspaces, semantic models (datasets), reports, dashboards, dataflows, apps | Over-provisioned workspace roles, RLS gaps, over-sharing, publish-to-web |
| S2 | **Power BI REST API** | api.powerbi.com — everything the UI does, plus admin/scanner APIs | Enumeration beyond intended visibility, executeQueries RLS abuse, embed token minting |
| S3 | **On-premises Data Gateway** | `PBIEgwService` Windows service bridging cloud → internal DBs | Stored-credential recovery (host compromise = all datasource creds), over-privileged service account, Kerberos SSO/delegation abuse |
| S4 | **Power BI Desktop artifacts** | `.pbix`/`.pbit`/`.pbip` files, local caches, autosave, DPAPI credential store | Full cached data disclosure, secrets in Power Query M code, internal hostnames |
| S5 | **Power BI Report Server (on-prem)** | Self-hosted SSRS-style portal (`/reports`, `/reportserver`) | Anonymous access, IDOR in REST API v2, cached credentials, direct render/export |
| S6 | **Identity layer (Entra ID)** | Licenses, tenant settings, service principals, guests, consent | Tenant-wide settings that weaken all of the above |

**Key documented behaviors that drive the whole methodology:**
1. **RLS is NOT applied to users holding Admin / Member / Contributor roles on a workspace** — only Viewers (and viewers with Build permission) are RLS-constrained. Over-provisioned roles = data exposure by design.
2. **Datasets reachable via the `executeQueries` REST API can be queried with DAX**, including an `impersonatedUserName` parameter (requires elevated access on the dataset; ignored when the model has no RLS).
3. **Gateway datasource credentials are only decryptable on the gateway host** — the cloud sends RSA-encrypted credentials; the private key lives in the gateway's local configuration protected by the gateway service account. Gateway host compromise ⇒ all stored datasource credentials.
4. **Publish-to-web** embeds are fully anonymous on the internet with **no RLS applied** when enabled.
5. A `.pbix` in import mode contains **the full cached dataset** plus the **entire Power Query (M) transformation code** — anyone with the file has the data.

