## 7. Phase 5 — Local & On-Prem Attacks (PowerShell + browser, 60–90 min)

### 5.1 PBIX hunting (workstation + accessible file shares/Teams/SharePoint)
```powershell
$roots = "$env:USERPROFILE\Documents","$env:USERPROFILE\Desktop","\\fileserver\share"   # adjust
foreach ($r in $roots) {
  Get-ChildItem $r -Recurse -Include *.pbix,*.pbit,*.pbip -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
    Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime
}
```
Teams/SharePoint: search `*.pbix` / "filetype:pbix" in SharePoint search UI.

### 5.2 PBIX autopsy (built-ins only — a pbix is a ZIP)
```powershell
Copy-Item .\report.pbix .\report.zip -Force
Expand-Archive .\report.zip -DestinationPath .\pbix -Force
Get-ChildItem .\pbix -Recurse | Select-Object FullName

# Power Query M code (connections, credentials, internal hosts):
Copy-Item .\pbix\DataMashup .\dm.zip -Force
Expand-Archive .\dm.zip -DestinationPath .\dm -Force
Get-Content .\dm\Formulas\Section1.m

# Report definition (UTF-16 JSON) — hunt secrets/URLs:
$raw = Get-Content .\pbix\Report\Layout -Encoding Unicode -Raw
[regex]::Matches($raw,'https?://[^\\"\s]+') | ForEach-Object {$_.Value} | Sort-Object -Unique
[regex]::Matches($raw,'(?i)(passw|secret|token|apikey|bearer)[^,}\]]{0,60}') | ForEach-Object {$_.Value} | Select-Object -Unique -First 40

# Live/DirectQuery models: read the connection file
Get-Content .\pbix\Connections
```
- `DataModel` (import mode) = full cached dataset — **open it in Power BI Desktop and every table is visible** (no creds needed for cached data).
- `DataMashup`/`Section1.m` = all M code — grep for `password=`, `apiKey`, `Token.Sys`, `Authorization`, internal hostnames.
- `Report/Layout` = schema (every column name), visual defs, embedded URLs.

### 5.3 Power BI Desktop workstation artifacts
```powershell
Get-Service | Where-Object { $_.DisplayName -match 'gateway|power\s*bi' } | Format-Table Name,DisplayName,Status
Get-ChildItem "$env:LOCALAPPDATA\Microsoft\Power BI Desktop" -Recurse -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue |
  Select-Object FullName, Length, LastWriteTime | Out-File pbi-desktop.txt
Get-ChildItem "$env:APPDATA\Microsoft\Windows\Recent" -Filter *.lnk | Where-Object { $_.Name -match '\.pb' }
```
Look for: `AutoSave` (unsaved report copies), `Temp*` model caches, any file named `*credential*` (DPAPI blobs — decryptable only in that user's context, which is the situation when you're on a user's box in a pentest).

### 5.4 On-premises data gateway (if you get access to a gateway host)
```powershell
Get-CimInstance Win32_Service | Where-Object { $_.PathName -match 'gateway|PBIEgw|PowerBI' } |
  Select-Object Name, StartName, State, PathName | Format-List          # <-- the SERVICE ACCOUNT (often over-privileged in AD)
Get-ChildItem 'C:\Program Files\On-premises data gateway' -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Select-Object Name,Length,LastWriteTime
net user <gateway-svc-account> /domain                                    # domain rights of the service account
```
Collect (copy, don't move): `GatewayInfo.json`, `GatewayConfig.json`, `Microsoft.PowerBI.DataMovement.Pipeline.GatewayCore.dll`, and any per-datasource config blobs. **Documented architecture: credentials for every datasource are RSA-encrypted and only decryptable on the gateway host with key material protected by the gateway service account — so gateway host admin access should be treated as "all datasource credentials".** If you have the gateway recovery key (obtain per ROE) stored credentials can be decrypted offline; otherwise record host + service account as the escalation path.
Kerberos SSO datasources (`singleSignOnType: Kerberos`) → check the service account's SPNs/delegation (`setspn -L <account>`); constrained/unconstrained delegation = domain escalation surface.

### 5.5 Power BI Report Server (on-prem, if in scope)
```text
Portal:    https://<host>/reports
Service:   https://<host>/reportserver
REST API:  https://<host>/reports/api/v2.0/Me, /Folders, /Reports, /PowerBIReports, /CatalogItems
```
- Browse `/reports` unauthenticated → expect 401; anything else is a finding.
- Authenticated: enumerate `/reports/api/v2.0/PowerBIReports` — compare against what the UI shows you; items viewable via direct API/path but hidden in UI = IDOR.
- Classic SSRS URL access: `https://<host>/reportserver?/Folder/Report&rs:Command=Render&rs:Format=CSV` (also try `EXCELOPENXML`, `PDF`) — render/export reports your role shouldn't reach.
- `GET /reportserver?/Folder/SharedDataSource&rs:Command=GetDataSourceDefinition` can leak connection strings on misconfigured instances.

